From 8a9e14c100586f68ee140280a2a07e369d70ff8b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 07:14:07 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08087/11362] Object perception moves held-out induction
 fidelity, but the metric it moves is unvalidated

exp6018 reported the object-perception A/B as unmeasurable: its pre-registered primary,
held-out exact-full-grid transition accuracy, was 0.0 in both arms on all 168 cells, so
zero per-game pairs were discordant and no sign test existed. This re-runs the A/B on the
CURRENT generator (gemma-4-31B-it-qat, one CUDA server for both arms) with the primary
switched to change_fidelity, pre-registered and hashed before the first LLM call.

Result on the pre-registered primary: 20 games, +15 / -4 / =1, two-sided sign p = 0.0192,
mean per-game delta +0.0721, bootstrap CI [+0.0102, +0.1316]. The >=3-gradable-row
co-primary agrees (14 games, p = 0.0225, same direction). exp6018's floor reproduces
exactly on the new generator: 1 of 317 graded changing transitions was predicted exactly
across all 124 cells.

Three things keep this from being a flag flip, and they are in the artifact rather than
implied. The A/A control was NOT byte-identical (1 of 4), so the pipeline is not exactly
reproducible; the pre-registered response is to report that as a floor, and the floor is
computed -- A/A |delta| max 0.0909 against a treatment mean |delta| of 0.1174, which the
effect clears. Within-arm replicate spread (mean 0.2599) is 3.6x the effect, so the result
exists only because replicates are averaged and games are paired. And there is no
dose-response: block size varies 5x across the roster and predicts nothing
(Spearman -0.074).

Adversarial review raised seven findings against the metric choice. Each was reproduced
against frozen data before being applied, and two did not reproduce as written:

  - The headroom artifact's validity anchor is circular. On tn36's frozen window every
    change is a single cell in row 1 -- a progress bar inside a uniform border strip, with
    the playfield starting at row 8 -- so change_fidelity is arithmetically forced to equal
    exact-match there, and their identical distinct-value sets show it. A corrigendum is
    recorded in this artifact rather than edited into that one, which its own harness would
    overwrite on rebuild.
  - That does NOT carry over to this A/B. tn36's window here has one gradable row changing
    four cells across the bar AND the playfield, where the coordinate-blind bar-ticker
    scores 0.2500, not 1.0000. Measured, not assumed -- the reviewer flagged it as inferred.
  - The reviewer's own 4-line demonstration engine scores 0.0196, not 1.0000; it recolours
    every 9 in the row at once and change_fidelity correctly charges it 862 spurious cells.

Applied: the four no-op/invention channels are now recorded and analysed (an engine perfect
on changing rows that hallucinates every no-op scores change_fidelity 1.0000 at 0.0714
exact accuracy, and spurious_changed_cells reads 0 on it) -- though the channel is
structurally dead on this roster, all 20 games having zero gradable no-op rows, which is
stated rather than left as a passing 0.0. Added a coordinate/action-blindness disqualifier
and two non-model baselines: identity scores 0.0 on all 19 rebuildable games, delta-replay
on 18 of 19, and the oracle reaches 1.0 on all 19.

Two independent second witnesses, derived differently from the run's own checks: the arms
differ by a pure insertion of the object block (common-prefix/suffix excision, and the
rebuilt prompts match the sha256 the run recorded), and no held-out line, and no held-out
START GRID, appears in either prompt. The 116 induced engines ship so the numbers are
recomputable without a GPU.

Known gaps, stated: HUD masking was off in both arms and cannot be reconstructed (the cells
record transitions, not frame masks); tr87's build_progress_window does not terminate, so
it has no post-hoc channels; and analyse.py was run mid-collection while wiring these
fixes, which is a deviation from the "analyse once" rule and is disclosed.

The open question this raises and cannot answer: on the frozen best-of-N corpus, both
plannable candidates sit in the bottom half of the change_fidelity ranking (ranks 13/31 and
18/31, one at exactly 0.0) while all six perfect-fidelity candidates are unplannable. n=2
establishes nothing, but it means a null OR a win on this metric should not yet be read as
a statement about agent capability.

Flag remains default off. No scored or online game was played.
